   "text": {
    "L1": "Helen Keller asks us to imagine having sight for only three days.",
    "L2": "She does this so each sight feels precious, not ordinary.",
    "L3": "Her main idea is that people who can see often take sight for granted.",
    "L4": "The faces of loved ones, a sunrise, and the stars are details that support this idea.",
    "L5": "Helen Keller was blind and deaf, which gives her reminder special force.",
    "L6": "For an imagined situation, use the pattern: If I had, I would see.",
    "L7": "The lesson is to notice and value our senses fully every day."
   },
